      "headline": "Why Does My Foot Hurt?",
      "keyInsight": "Modern medicine treats running injuries like freak car crashes that need expensive armor. If your knee aches, the standard advice is simple: buy heavier shoes, take painkillers, or give up. Yet running is the single athletic activity every human body was sculpted to perform. Lions do not get hamstring pulls chasing gazelles across the savannah. Birds do not get shin splints when migrating across oceans. Only modern humans manage to injure themselves doing the basic movement that kept our ancestors alive. We turned a natural survival skill into an orthopedic medical emergency. When an athletic runner cannot jog three miles without agonizing pain, the fault is not our anatomy. The culprit is how we modern runners move and what we strap onto our feet.",
      "content": "Imagine buying a brand new sports car that blows an axle every fifty miles. You take it to the mechanic, and he gives you a strange answer. He tells you the car was simply never built to drive on roads. That sounds utterly absurd, yet sports doctors deliver that exact verdict to runners every single day.\n\nChristopher McDougall was an active journalist who kept breaking down. Every time he jogged, sharp pain shot through his arches. His doctor gave him a grim prescription: stay on the sofa and buy a bicycle. Running on hard pavement, the doctor insisted, is like pounding an egg with a steel hammer. The human foot is supposedly too fragile for repeated ground impact.\n\nThat diagnosis felt deeply suspicious to a curious mind. Our ancestors hunted wild game on their feet for hundreds of thousands of years. They chased prey across jagged rocks and baking dirt without high-tech padded sneakers. If running naturally shattered joints, our entire species would have died out long ago. A deer does not need custom orthotics to sprint through a forest.\n\nSomething was clearly backwards in modern fitness logic. Nature rarely designs an animal that hurts itself doing basic locomotion. The agonizing pain in our feet is not an inevitable design flaw. It is a blinking red warning light telling us that modern humans have completely forgotten how to move.",
